// An interface signature does not take a trailing `;`. Roadmap B15.
//
// This is the fixture that did not exist, and its absence is the whole story: for seventy
// versions stage-0 ACCEPTED the `;` form and stage-1 refused it, so the same program compiled
// with one compiler and failed to parse with the other. The differential runs both compilers
// over the suite and reports every disagreement — but it can only disagree about a program
// somebody wrote, and nobody had written this one.
//
// A divergence in what is ACCEPTED is the direction that matters. A program refused by both is
// merely unwritten; a program one compiler takes and the other rejects means the language has
// two definitions.
//
// Fixed by refusing it in stage-0 rather than by allowing it in stage-1, and the rest of the
// language is what decides that: a class body refuses a stray `;`, and so does an enum. The
// interface was the only declaration body that took one. An optional separator is a second
// spelling of one thing, and this language does not keep those.
interface Predicate {
    function test(self, x: Int) -> Bool;
}

class Positive { tag: Int }

implement Predicate for Positive {
    function (self) test(x: Int) -> Bool { return x > 0; }
}

let p: Positive = Positive { tag: 1 };
let d: dynamic Predicate = p;
print(d.test(3));
